+# Fork Example
+
+Run a side investigation in a second pi process that already knows the
+conversation, without stopping the current task.
+
+```
+User: implement the retry helper and check whether the other call sites have the same bug
+Assistant: ... (calls fork with task "check the other call sites for the same bug")
+Assistant: ... keeps working ...
+Fork 01924f0c-... started in the background. Its result arrives as a message when it finishes.
+... later, in the same conversation:
+the other call sites go through the same helper, but only webhook.ts passes the wrong timeout
+```
+
+## Install
+
+List the directory under `extensions` in the agent directory's `settings.json`
+(`~/.pi/agent/settings.json`), so every pi process that uses those settings
+loads it:
+
+```json
+{
+  "extensions": ["/absolute/path/to/examples/extensions/fork"]
+}
+```
+
+Or copy/symlink it into a directory pi auto-discovers:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p ~/.pi/agent/extensions/fork
+ln -sf "$(pwd)/packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/fork/index.ts" ~/.pi/agent/extensions/fork/index.ts
+```
+
+Installing it through settings (rather than `pi -e`) matters: the fork process
+loads the same settings, and only then does it know that it is a fork.
+
+## Tool
+
+`fork({ task })` starts a fork and returns once its process has started:
+
+- The result text names the fork id, `details` is `{ forkId }`.
+- An empty or whitespace-only task is rejected and starts nothing.
+- Forks run in parallel; one assistant message may start several.
+
+## What a fork inherits
+
+The fork's session file is created in the parent's session directory and its
+header points at the parent session (`parentSession`). Its first request to the
+model starts with:
+
+1. the messages of the request that produced the forking assistant message
+   (a compacted session contributes its compacted view),
+2. that assistant message,
+3. a tool result for every tool call of that assistant message, and
+4. the task, which is the fork's only new instruction.
+
+The fork runs `pi -p --session <file> --model <parent model> --thinking <parent
+level> -- <task>`: the same CLI, environment, agent directory, settings,
+extensions and providers as the parent. It has no `fork` tool, works without a
+user, and finishes when its agent run ends. Nothing the parent does after the
+fork reaches the fork.
+
+A tool call that the parent made in the same assistant message has no result
+yet when the fork starts (pi runs tool calls in parallel). Its recorded result
+says so instead of inventing one, so the provider still sees a valid
+conversation.
+
+## What the parent sees
+
+When a fork finishes, exactly one custom message appears in the parent session:
+
+```json
+{
+  "customType": "fork-result",
+  "display": true,
+  "content": "the fork's final assistant text, or the failure reason",
+  "details": {
+    "forkId": "01924f0c-...",
+    "status": "completed",
+    "sessionFile": "/home/you/.pi/agent/sessions/--...--/2026-01-01T00-00-00-000Z_....jsonl"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- If the parent is idle, the result starts a new run.
+- If the parent is running, its model sees the result in a later request of the
+  same run; running tools are not interrupted.
+- If the run is aborted (Escape), queued messages are discarded by pi, so the
+  extension re-adds an undelivered result once the parent is idle. The reader
+  of the session is the source of truth: a result counts as delivered when its
+  `customType: "fork-result"` entry is in the session, so it is delivered once.
+- `sessionFile` is omitted when the fork never created a session file.
+- A fork whose process exits abnormally, is killed, or whose last model response
+  errored produces one result with `status: "failed"` and the reason (the
+  provider's error message for a model error).
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+- A fork belongs to the session that started it. On `quit`, `/new`, `/resume`,
+  `/fork` or `/clone` its forks get `SIGTERM` (then `SIGKILL` after 2s) and
+  deliver nothing.
+- If the parent process dies without shutting down, the fork notices that its
+  parent is gone and exits within a second.
+- `/reload` keeps the session alive, so forks keep running. The replacement
+  extension instance adopts them from the `fork` custom entries in the session
+  and delivers each result once.
+
+## Notes
+
+- With `--no-session` the parent has no session file, so the fork's header has
+  no `parentSession` and its session file lands in the default session
+  directory for the cwd. Everything else works the same.
+- The fork inherits the parent's model and thinking level, and the session's
+  tool settings. Tool restrictions passed only as CLI flags to the parent
+  (`--tools`, `--exclude-tools`) are not forwarded.
+- Forks are ordinary sessions, so they show up in the session selector with the
+  parent session recorded as their origin.
